  "mainEntity": {
    "name": "What Grit Sandpaper Should You Use for Drywall?",
    "text": "What Grit Sandpaper Should You Use for Drywall?",
    "@type": "Question",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "text": "Choosing the right sandpaper grit for drywall is a common question, and getting it right makes a huge difference in your final finish. Generally, you don't want to go too coarse, or you risk gouging the compound. For initial smoothing and knocking down the main ridges after mudding, a medium-grit sandpaper, typically in the range of 100 to 150, is a good starting point. This helps to efficiently remove the excess material without being overly aggressive. Once you've got the major imperfections smoothed out, you'll want to switch to a finer grit for the final pass. Something in the 180 to 220 grit range is perfect for achieving that super smooth, almost invisible finish. This finer grit will leave the surface perfectly prepared for priming and painting. It's really important to sand only after the compound is completely dry; otherwise, you'll just make a mess and possibly damage the surface. Remember, the goal is to blend everything seamlessly, not to sand through to the tape! If you're ever unsure about the best approach for your specific project, or if you want that perfectly smooth, professional-grade finish, don't hesitate to reach out to us. We’re here to help you get it looking just right!",
      "@type": "Answer",
      "author": {
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC",
        "@type": "Organization"
      }
    }
  }
